// lalu — the LU factorisation with partial pivoting, which is the one
// every other function in this package falls back to.
//
// Every body is a `todo()`.  The signatures are the design, and the
// compiler checks them as claims; see the README for the stamp.
//
// WHAT IT IS.  `P A = L U`: a permutation, a unit-lower-triangular
// factor and an upper-triangular one.  Gaussian elimination with the
// largest remaining entry in each column chosen as the pivot, which is
// what makes it numerically stable — without pivoting, a matrix as
// benign as `[[0, 1], [1, 0]]` divides by zero on the first step.
//
// LAPACK: `dgetrf` factors, `dgetrs` solves, `dgecon` estimates the
// condition number, `dgetri` inverts.  Every function here names the
// routine it mirrors, and the README says why that matters: a result
// that disagrees with LAPACK is this package's fault, and a reader has
// to be able to find the thing to compare against.
//
// THE LOAD-BEARING DECISION, AND IT IS THIS PACKAGE'S WHOLE SHAPE: A
// DECOMPOSITION IS A VALUE THAT HOLDS ITS FACTORS.  `factor` answers an
// `LaLu`; `solve` takes one.  A caller solving `A x = b` for fifty
// different `b` — which is what a simulation step, a Kalman filter and
// a Newton iteration all do — factors once and solves fifty times, at
// `n³/3` for the factorisation and `n²` for each solve.  An API of one
// `solve(A, b)` function would have hidden that and done the cubic work
// fifty times.
//
// And a value, not a handle: no `init`, no `free`, no mutation.  An
// `LaLu` can live in a struct beside the matrix it came from, be
// copied, and be used from two places; it cannot be stale, because
// nothing changes it.  That is also what keeps this package at `core`
// with nothing carried between calls.
//
// THE PACKED FORM IS NOT PUBLISHED, ON PURPOSE.  LAPACK stores `L` and
// `U` in the same array, because `L`'s diagonal is all ones and there
// is exactly enough room.  That is the right storage and the wrong API:
// a caller who wants `L` should get `L`.  So `LaLu` holds the packed
// array as its field, and `lower`, `upper` and `permutation` are
// functions that unpack — a copy each, paid only by the caller who
// asks, which is almost nobody, because `solve` reads the packed form
// directly.

use ndfloat

// LaLu is the LU factorisation of a square matrix, with its pivots.
//
// Build it with `factor` and keep it for as long as the matrix lives.
// The fields are public because a reader checking this against
// `dgetrf`'s documented output needs to see the packed layout, and
// because a test can then assert the pivot sequence directly rather
// than only through a solve.
//
// ```novo
// use ndfloat
// use lalu
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match ndfloat.eye(3)
//         Ok(a) =>
//             match lalu.factor(a)
//                 Ok(lu) => println("${lalu.order(lu)}")   // 3
//                 Err(e) => println(e.message())
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub struct LaLu
    // `L` and `U` in one `n x n` matrix, LAPACK's packing: the strict
    // lower triangle is `L`'s (its diagonal is all ones and is not
    // stored), and the upper triangle including the diagonal is `U`.
    packed: NdFloat
    // The row swaps, in the order they were applied: `pivots[i]` is the
    // row that was exchanged with row `i` at step `i`.  LAPACK's
    // `ipiv`, zero-based rather than one-based.
    pivots: [Int]
    // How many row exchanges were actually performed — even means the
    // permutation's determinant is +1 and odd means -1, which is the
    // sign `determinant` needs and the only thing it needs the
    // permutation for.
    swaps: Int

// The LU factorisation of a square matrix, with partial pivoting.
//
// LAPACK `dgetrf`.  `LaNotMatrix` for an array that is not rank 2,
// `LaNotSquare` for a non-square one, `LaEmptyMatrix` for a zero
// dimension, and `LaSingular` with the column and the pivot when a
// column is entirely zero below the diagonal.
//
// A matrix that is merely ill-conditioned factors fine and says nothing
// — the factorisation cannot tell, which is what `lanorm.condition` is
// for. `LaSingular` here means exactly zero to within the working
// precision, not "nearly singular".
pub fn factor(a: NdFloat) -> Result<LaLu, LaFault> []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.factor")

// How many rows the factored matrix had.
pub fn order(lu: LaLu) -> Int []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.order")

// The unit-lower-triangular factor `L`, unpacked into its own `n x n`
// matrix with ones on the diagonal.
//
// A copy.  See the module comment: the packed form is the storage and
// this is the API.
pub fn lower(lu: LaLu) -> NdFloat []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.lower")

// The upper-triangular factor `U`, unpacked.
pub fn upper(lu: LaLu) -> NdFloat []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.upper")

// The permutation `P` as an `n x n` matrix of zeros and ones, so that
// `P A = L U` can be checked directly.
//
// A permutation matrix is a wasteful way to store a permutation and an
// excellent way to verify one, which is the whole reason it is here:
// the suite multiplies the three factors and compares against the
// original.
pub fn permutation(lu: LaLu) -> NdFloat []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.permutation")

// The permutation as the index list `perm[i] = j`, meaning row `j` of
// the original is row `i` of `P A`.
//
// What a caller applies to a right-hand side without building a matrix.
pub fn permutation_indices(lu: LaLu) -> [Int] []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.permutation_indices")

// `x` such that `A x = b`, for a vector `b`.
//
// LAPACK `dgetrs`.  Forward substitution through `L` and back
// substitution through `U`, at `n²` — which is the whole point of
// keeping the factorisation: fifty right-hand sides cost fifty times
// `n²` and not fifty times `n³/3`.
//
// `LaDimensionMismatch` when `b` is not `n` long, `LaNotVector` when it
// is not rank 1.
//
// ```novo
// use ndfloat
// use lalu
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match ndfloat.eye(2)
//         Ok(a) =>
//             match lalu.factor(a)
//                 Ok(lu) =>
//                     match ndfloat.of_list([1.0, 2.0], [2])
//                         Ok(b) =>
//                             match lalu.solve(lu, b)
//                                 Ok(x)  => println("${ndfloat.size(x)}")   // 2
//                                 Err(e) => println(e.message())
//                         Err(e) => println(e.message())
//                 Err(e) => println(e.message())
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn solve(lu: LaLu, b: NdFloat) -> Result<NdFloat, LaFault> []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.solve")

// `X` such that `A X = B`, for a matrix `B` of several right-hand
// sides at once.
//
// The same `dgetrs`, and the call a caller with fifty right-hand sides
// should reach for instead of fifty `solve`s: one pass over the
// factors, one allocation, and the inner loops run over columns rather
// than over calls.
pub fn solve_matrix(lu: LaLu, b: NdFloat) -> Result<NdFloat, LaFault> []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.solve_matrix")

// The determinant of the factored matrix: the product of `U`'s
// diagonal, with the sign of the permutation.
//
// Not a separate algorithm — the determinant of an LU factorisation is
// a single pass down a diagonal, which is why nobody should ever
// compute one by cofactor expansion and why this is the only
// determinant this package has.
//
// Beware the overflow that is inherent rather than this package's: the
// determinant of a 100 x 100 matrix of entries around 10 is around
// 10^100, and the product runs through every intermediate on the way.
// `log_abs_determinant` is the call for anything large.
pub fn determinant(lu: LaLu) -> Float []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.determinant")

// The natural logarithm of the absolute determinant, and its sign, as a
// pair.
//
// The call a statistician actually wants: a Gaussian log-likelihood
// needs `log|det Σ|` and never `det Σ`, and computing the second and
// taking a logarithm overflows for any covariance matrix of interesting
// size.  Summing the logarithms of `U`'s diagonal does not.
//
// `sign` is +1, -1 or 0, and `log_abs` is `-inf` when the sign is 0.
pub fn log_abs_determinant(lu: LaLu) -> LaLogDet []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.log_abs_determinant")

// LaLogDet is a log-determinant and the sign it lost.
//
// A struct rather than a tuple because `d.0` and `d.1` three lines
// later is how a sign becomes a magnitude.
pub struct LaLogDet
    // +1, -1, or 0 for a singular matrix.
    sign: Float
    // `log|det A|`, and `-inf` when the determinant is zero.
    log_abs: Float

// The inverse of the factored matrix.
//
// LAPACK `dgetri`.  Present because callers want it and prefaced with
// the warning every numerical text gives: **to solve `A x = b`, use
// `solve`.**  Forming the inverse and multiplying is three times the
// work and loses a digit or two of accuracy, and the only reasons to
// have an explicit inverse are that a formula genuinely needs its
// entries — a covariance matrix's, a Hat matrix's — or that it is being
// displayed.
//
// `LaSingular` when the factorisation's `U` has a zero on its diagonal.
pub fn inverse(lu: LaLu) -> Result<NdFloat, LaFault> []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.inverse")

// The product `L U` with the permutation undone — the original matrix,
// reconstructed from the factors.
//
// A round trip, and the assertion every decomposition owes: the factors
// multiply back to what was factored, to within a tolerance the README
// names.  It costs `n³` and exists for tests and for a reader checking
// a factorisation by hand, not for a hot path.
pub fn reconstruct(lu: LaLu) -> NdFloat []
    todo("linalg-nv.lalu.reconstruct")
